    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Image processing technologies and mathematical methods are introduced to quantify soil crack patterns that appear in clay soils under drying conditions. The quantification of crack images mainly involves the following three steps: image segmentation, crack identification and measurement. In the first step, a crack image is converted to a binary image; the spots are removed and the crack breaks are fused. In the second step, the medial axis of the crack network is extracted from the binary image, the nodes and crack segments are identified. In the third step, geometric parameters that characterize the main features of crack patterns are calculated and can be used to describe the evolution of the crack network during drying. The thresholds used in the operations are specified by cluster analysis and other innovative methods, by which the artificial errors are reduced. With the application of the proposed image analysis methods, three crack patterns obtained under different test temperatures were quantified, and several geometric parameters were determined to implicate the temperature dependence of soil cracking behavior.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
